WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.288
Welcome to Module 6 of the
HR-Driven Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.288 --> 00:00:06.537
time separations from the logs.

3
00:00:06.537 --> 00:00:10.032
This is Gate 6 of the
implementation guide, separation,

4
00:00:10.032 --> 00:00:13.449
and it goes with Chapter 06
of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:13.449 --> 00:00:17.334
When a person separates, two things
have to be shown: that access was

6
00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:21.712
blocked within the approved interval,
and that the credential was revoked.

7
00:00:21.712 --> 00:00:25.717
In this module you'll join the records
each system keeps about a leaver,

8
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:29.583
compute two intervals for every one
of them, separation-to-block and

9
00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:33.450
separation-to-revocation, and find
the separations that went wrong.

10
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.125
You'll run the measurement on
fictional leavers, with no tenant.

11
00:00:37.125 --> 00:00:40.640
The module takes about
forty-five minutes with its lab.

12
00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:45.977
Why does this gate need code?

13
00:00:45.977 --> 00:00:49.408
Your ISSO has to show that each
leaver's access was blocked

14
00:00:49.408 --> 00:00:53.339
within the approved interval and
that the credential was revoked.

15
00:00:53.339 --> 00:00:55.902
No Microsoft report gives that number.

16
00:00:55.902 --> 00:00:59.427
Lifecycle Workflows records
that a leaver run completed.

17
00:00:59.427 --> 00:01:02.341
The audit log records when
the account was disabled.

18
00:01:02.341 --> 00:01:05.339
The card system records
the card's revocation,

19
00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:08.890
and Intune records the devices
being retired or wiped.

20
00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.715
Each lives in its own place.

21
00:01:10.715 --> 00:01:16.193
So in this module you join those records
for each leaver, compute the two intervals,

22
00:01:16.193 --> 00:01:20.865
and find the separations that went
wrong: an entry made late, a re-enable,

23
00:01:20.865 --> 00:01:26.300
a failed run, a card revoked too late, a
delete before the devices were retired.

24
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.963
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of the module.

25
00:01:32.963 --> 00:01:36.400
You'll describe the leaver
order: block first,

26
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.024
revoke in parallel,
reconcile, and dispose last.

27
00:01:40.024 --> 00:01:44.072
You'll define separation-to-block
and separation-to-revocation,

28
00:01:44.072 --> 00:01:46.363
and say which records each one reads.

29
00:01:46.363 --> 00:01:50.975
You'll run the measurement and explain its
findings, including the catch-up window.

30
00:01:50.975 --> 00:01:53.290
And you'll explain why
the tenant's workflow

31
00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.863
schedule belongs inside
the approved interval.

32
00:01:55.863 --> 00:01:58.513
This module builds on three earlier ones.

33
00:01:58.513 --> 00:02:01.396
Module 1: the pipeline
sends active false no

34
00:02:01.396 --> 00:02:04.200
later than the leaver
workflow's first run.

35
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.437
Module 3: the leaver and
post-separation workflow files.

36
00:02:08.437 --> 00:02:14.120
And Module 5: a card's revocation reaches
sign-in through the revocation list.

37
00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.270
Unit 1 reads the guide's Step 6.

38
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.873
It orders the leaver as
a sequence: block first,

39
00:02:23.873 --> 00:02:27.570
revoke in parallel,
reconcile, and dispose last.

40
00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.032
It times the leave date late in the day.

41
00:02:30.032 --> 00:02:34.985
It coordinates the two writers, so
one can't undo the other; in the lab,

42
00:02:34.985 --> 00:02:40.208
a re-enable after the disable with no
cancellation in HR is rule S T five.

43
00:02:40.208 --> 00:02:44.869
It builds an emergency path, whose
on-demand runs are logged as changes.

44
00:02:44.869 --> 00:02:49.032
It catches separations that were
entered late, which is S T three.

45
00:02:49.032 --> 00:02:53.144
It verifies the block, which
is S T one and S T two.

46
00:02:53.144 --> 00:02:58.995
And it disposes last: deleting a user before
the devices are retired is S T eight.

47
00:02:58.995 --> 00:03:03.926
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
HR-Driven Identity book has the canonical

48
00:03:03.926 --> 00:03:08.784
leaver sequence, and Chapter 05 of Building
the Attribute-Driven Migration has

49
00:03:08.784 --> 00:03:11.581
already built the custom
task extension that

50
00:03:11.581 --> 00:03:14.450
revokes the card and retires the devices.

51
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.588
Two timing facts shape the measurement.

52
00:03:20.588 --> 00:03:22.326
First, late entries.

53
00:03:22.326 --> 00:03:25.641
Learn says a scheduled workflow
still processes a user

54
00:03:25.641 --> 00:03:28.812
whose trigger date passed no
more than three days ago.

55
00:03:28.812 --> 00:03:32.387
Past three days, the scheduled
trigger skips the user.

56
00:03:32.387 --> 00:03:35.351
Chapter 08 of the HR-Driven Identity book

57
00:03:35.351 --> 00:03:38.925
explains why a separation
entered late misses it.

58
00:03:38.925 --> 00:03:44.015
So rule S T three flags any separation
entered more than three days after its

59
00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:47.974
effective date: a Finding if
nobody ran the leaver on demand,

60
00:03:47.974 --> 00:03:49.913
and only a Warn if someone did.

61
00:03:49.913 --> 00:03:51.925
Second, the tenant's schedule.

62
00:03:51.925 --> 00:03:57.063
Lifecycle Workflows runs on a schedule, and
that can delay a scheduled leaver's run.

63
00:03:57.063 --> 00:03:59.229
The wait is part of separation-to-block,

64
00:03:59.229 --> 00:04:03.088
so the guide says the schedule
belongs inside the approved interval.

65
00:04:03.088 --> 00:04:06.090
Set the threshold and
the schedule together.

66
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.315
Unit 2 defines the two intervals.

67
00:04:12.315 --> 00:04:14.777
Both start at the HR effective time.

68
00:04:14.777 --> 00:04:18.088
Separation-to-block ends
when the person was blocked,

69
00:04:18.088 --> 00:04:21.549
and the code takes that as the
later of two audit records:

70
00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:25.312
the first Disable account, and
the first session revocation,

71
00:04:25.312 --> 00:04:29.827
which the audit log calls Update S T
S Refresh Token Valid From Timestamp.

72
00:04:29.827 --> 00:04:32.991
Disabled but still holding
a session isn't blocked.

73
00:04:32.991 --> 00:04:36.772
Separation-to-revocation ends
at the last of three things:

74
00:04:36.772 --> 00:04:40.790
the block, the card system's
revocation and the device actions.

75
00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:44.485
To compute them, the measurement
reads the HR separations,

76
00:04:44.485 --> 00:04:49.488
the audit records and leaver run results
exported from Graph, the card system's

77
00:04:49.488 --> 00:04:54.415
revocations, Intune's device actions,
and the list of privileged role holders.

78
00:04:54.415 --> 00:04:55.627
It never guesses.

79
00:04:55.627 --> 00:04:59.840
A leaver missing either audit record
is not blocked, and that's a finding.

80
00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.990
A cancelled separation
isn't measured at all.

81
00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.202
Here are the nine rules.

82
00:05:08.202 --> 00:05:12.777
S T one and S T nine compare the
two intervals with their thresholds.

83
00:05:12.777 --> 00:05:17.378
S T two: no block was recorded,
or there's no account to measure.

84
00:05:17.378 --> 00:05:21.678
S T three is the late entry, past
the three-day catch-up window.

85
00:05:21.678 --> 00:05:24.456
S T four: a card that
couldn't be collected was

86
00:05:24.456 --> 00:05:27.465
revoked more than eighteen
hours after notice.

87
00:05:27.465 --> 00:05:32.724
S T five: the account was re-enabled after
the disable, with no cancellation in HR.

88
00:05:32.724 --> 00:05:37.740
S T six is a warning for a privileged
leaver, because the disable task doesn't

89
00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.248
support users with role assignments,

90
00:05:40.248 --> 00:05:43.403
so someone has to confirm
the verified path ran.

91
00:05:43.403 --> 00:05:48.052
S T seven: a leaver run failed,
and goes to the exception queue.

92
00:05:48.052 --> 00:05:53.515
And S T eight: the user was deleted before
the device retire or wipe completed.

93
00:05:53.515 --> 00:05:55.520
Disposal comes last.

94
00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:02.107
Not every number in the
rules is a requirement.

95
00:06:02.107 --> 00:06:03.333
Two are samples.

96
00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:07.228
The four-hour separation-to-block
threshold and the twenty-four-hour

97
00:06:07.228 --> 00:06:10.236
separation-to-revocation
threshold stand in for the

98
00:06:10.236 --> 00:06:13.244
agency's organization-defined
periods under P S four.

99
00:06:13.244 --> 00:06:15.920
The agency approves them or replaces them.

100
00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.895
Two are fixed by their sources.

101
00:06:17.895 --> 00:06:22.972
The eighteen hours for an uncollected card
is FIPS two oh one dash three's rule:

102
00:06:22.972 --> 00:06:28.445
a card that can't be collected is terminated
within eighteen hours of notification.

103
00:06:28.445 --> 00:06:31.009
And the three-day catch-up
window is Learn's,

104
00:06:31.009 --> 00:06:34.745
the window in which a scheduled
workflow still picks up a user.

105
00:06:34.745 --> 00:06:37.770
So the tool measures,
and the agency decides.

106
00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:42.820
Approve the block and revocation periods,
and set the tenant schedule inside them.

107
00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:47.295
Try it.

108
00:06:47.295 --> 00:06:50.788
Lab 1 runs in the h r
lifecycle examples folder,

109
00:06:50.788 --> 00:06:53.545
on fictional leavers, with no tenant.

110
00:06:53.545 --> 00:06:58.341
Step one lists what the reader would call
in a real tenant: two GET calls, the

111
00:06:58.341 --> 00:07:02.507
directory audits and the leaver
workflow's processing results,

112
00:07:02.507 --> 00:07:04.158
for two read permissions.

113
00:07:04.158 --> 00:07:09.527
Step two runs the measurement over
every input: the separations, the users,

114
00:07:09.527 --> 00:07:13.577
the audit records, the run
results, the card revocations,

115
00:07:13.577 --> 00:07:16.482
the device actions and the role holders.

116
00:07:16.482 --> 00:07:21.544
It reports seven separated leavers,
one cancelled, and one never blocked.

117
00:07:21.544 --> 00:07:24.872
The median separation-to-block
is a quarter of an hour,

118
00:07:24.872 --> 00:07:28.502
but the ninetieth percentile
is five point five two hours,

119
00:07:28.502 --> 00:07:32.283
over the four-hour sample, and
the worst is nearly eighty-five.

120
00:07:32.283 --> 00:07:35.180
Revocation is worse:
a ninetieth percentile

121
00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.921
of a hundred ten hours
against twenty-four.

122
00:07:37.921 --> 00:07:41.520
Ten Findings, two Warns, exit code two.

123
00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:49.870
Step three prints the findings,
one line each: twelve of them.

124
00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:51.795
Read them as stories about people.

125
00:07:51.795 --> 00:07:56.820
Casey was blocked after five and a half
hours, over the threshold, and Casey's card,

126
00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:58.571
which couldn't be collected,

127
00:07:58.571 --> 00:08:02.682
was revoked twenty-three hours
after notice, past FIPS's eighteen.

128
00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:06.289
Drew's separation was entered
six point eight days late,

129
00:08:06.289 --> 00:08:10.445
nobody ran the leaver on demand,
and Drew was never blocked at all.

130
00:08:10.445 --> 00:08:14.622
Harper was re-enabled after the
disable with no cancellation in HR,

131
00:08:14.622 --> 00:08:16.520
and Harper's leaver run failed.

132
00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:20.745
Kai holds a privileged role, and the
disable task doesn't support users

133
00:08:20.745 --> 00:08:24.970
with role assignments, so someone
has to confirm the verified path ran.

134
00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.508
And Blake was deleted before
the device retire completed.

135
00:08:28.508 --> 00:08:32.354
Emery shows the other side
of S T three: entered late,

136
00:08:32.354 --> 00:08:36.180
but caught by an on-demand
run, so it's only a Warn.

137
00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:43.218
Step four cuts four columns
from the intervals file:

138
00:08:43.218 --> 00:08:46.430
the person number, the two
intervals and the status.

139
00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:48.418
There's a header and eight rows.

140
00:08:48.418 --> 00:08:49.542
Six are measured.

141
00:08:49.542 --> 00:08:52.882
One, person one hundred three
oh three, is not blocked,

142
00:08:52.882 --> 00:08:56.454
and its interval columns are
empty rather than guessed.

143
00:08:56.454 --> 00:09:01.718
One, one hundred three oh seven, is a
cancelled separation, so it isn't measured.

144
00:09:01.718 --> 00:09:06.477
Look at one hundred three oh eight: blocked
in six minutes, but not fully revoked

145
00:09:06.477 --> 00:09:08.675
for three hundred ninety-five hours,

146
00:09:08.675 --> 00:09:11.604
because the card and the
devices came much later.

147
00:09:11.604 --> 00:09:16.530
Step five runs the tests, fifty-seven
passed, and deletes the out folder.

148
00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:19.946
Module 7 rebuilds this output
and reads its summary,

149
00:09:19.946 --> 00:09:25.400
where the two ninetieth percentiles
become two of its six lifecycle metrics.

150
00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:33.471
Lab 2 moves to your own test tenant,
never production, with Lifecycle

151
00:09:33.471 --> 00:09:38.100
Workflows licensed and a leaver workflow
in the workbook's leaver order.

152
00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.940
The reader app holds a
certificate and just the

153
00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:43.925
two permissions from step
one, with admin consent.

154
00:09:43.925 --> 00:09:48.650
You make two test leavers, one scheduled
and one run on demand, export the logs,

155
00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:53.300
and confirm which audit activity the
session revoke task actually writes,

156
00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.150
because the measurement
depends on that name.

157
00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:57.150
Then you measure.

158
00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.918
Expect each test user's
separation-to-block

159
00:09:59.918 --> 00:10:02.687
to include the wait for
the tenant schedule.

160
00:10:02.687 --> 00:10:06.087
That's the lesson from Unit
1, now in your own numbers.

161
00:10:06.087 --> 00:10:08.343
To back it out, remove the leave dates,

162
00:10:08.343 --> 00:10:12.703
re-enable or restore the users if you
need them, remove the permissions,

163
00:10:12.703 --> 00:10:16.312
and delete the reader app, its
certificate and the folders.

164
00:10:16.312 --> 00:10:18.820
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

165
00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:23.957
The federal requirement.

166
00:10:23.957 --> 00:10:28.358
In NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three terms,

167
00:10:28.358 --> 00:10:33.534
this gate meets P S four, personnel
termination; A C two, enhancement three,

168
00:10:33.534 --> 00:10:37.329
disable accounts; P S seven
for contractors; and A C two,

169
00:10:37.329 --> 00:10:42.333
enhancement thirteen, disabling
accounts for high-risk individuals.

170
00:10:42.333 --> 00:10:46.315
It also meets FIPS two oh one dash
three's rule that a card that

171
00:10:46.315 --> 00:10:51.094
can't be collected is terminated
within eighteen hours of notification.

172
00:10:51.094 --> 00:10:55.261
The evidence an ISSO keeps
for Gate 6: the intervals,

173
00:10:55.261 --> 00:11:00.420
the summary and the findings, with
their manifest, for every run.

174
00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.229
The exported workflow runs, audit records,

175
00:11:03.229 --> 00:11:06.520
card revocations and device
actions behind them.

176
00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:08.832
The contractor rows of the intervals.

177
00:11:08.832 --> 00:11:12.332
The notice and revocation
times for uncollected cards.

178
00:11:12.332 --> 00:11:16.800
And the emergency path's
on-demand runs, logged as changes.

179
00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:24.050
Check yourself with two questions
from the module's knowledge check.

180
00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:28.012
First: what does the code take as
the moment a leaver was blocked?

181
00:11:28.012 --> 00:11:30.832
The answer is b: the
later of the first Disable

182
00:11:30.832 --> 00:11:34.413
account record and the first
session revocation record.

183
00:11:34.413 --> 00:11:36.635
Not the workflow's completion time,

184
00:11:36.635 --> 00:11:40.699
and not the HR effective time,
which is where the interval starts.

185
00:11:40.699 --> 00:11:44.627
Second: a separation is entered
in HR more than three days

186
00:11:44.627 --> 00:11:48.888
after its effective date, and
nobody runs the leaver on demand.

187
00:11:48.888 --> 00:11:50.550
What does the measurement report?

188
00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:53.049
The answer is b: an S T three Finding,

189
00:11:53.049 --> 00:11:57.600
because past three days the
scheduled trigger skips the user.

190
00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.587
With an on-demand run,
it would be only a Warn.

191
00:12:00.587 --> 00:12:05.820
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that teaches it.

192
00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:10.395
To sum up.

193
00:12:10.395 --> 00:12:13.471
You put the leaver in order: block first,

194
00:12:13.471 --> 00:12:17.220
revoke in parallel,
reconcile, and dispose last.

195
00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:21.179
You defined separation-to-block
and separation-to-revocation,

196
00:12:21.179 --> 00:12:23.082
and the records each one reads.

197
00:12:23.082 --> 00:12:27.606
You measured fictional leavers and
found the ones that were blocked late,

198
00:12:27.606 --> 00:12:30.869
never blocked, re-enabled,
failed, or deleted too early.

199
00:12:30.869 --> 00:12:34.883
And you saw why the tenant schedule
belongs inside the approved interval.

200
00:12:34.883 --> 00:12:41.170
Next is Module 7, the last in the path:
find lifecycle drift and file the evidence.

201
00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:45.772
It writes one read-only run of
six drift classes and six metrics,

202
00:12:45.772 --> 00:12:49.135
reads this module's
percentiles as two of them,

203
00:12:49.135 --> 00:12:53.340
and packages each run with a
hash chain an ISSO can verify.
